From: SamuriHL <samurihl@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Nov 2026 12:10:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] bdj: an Ixc call into a callee destroyed during the call fails fast

A remote call whose callee xlet ends while the call runs never
answers: the caller joins its remote thread with no bound, so the
caller's own destroyXlet (and with it BDJAppProxy.release()) waits for
the 5 s STOP timeout and the Ixc thread's 1 s wait before the shutdown
goes on. On Universal discs (M3GAN 2.0: UniversalTitleBoundXlet calling
TitleChangeHandler.titleDestroyed() on the title xlet stopped with it)
every disc stop and every Kodi quit with the menu up paid about 6.5 s.

The callee's context now counts the ends of its xlet (destroyXlet
returned, a CMD_NOTIFY_DESTROYED, exitXlet). The caller records the
count at the call and joins in 100 ms steps; when it moves, or the
callee's context is released, it interrupts the remote thread and
throws RemoteException("callee destroyed during remote execution"), as
invoke() already refuses a call into a released callee. The proxy's
state cannot tell: a destroyed proxy goes from DESTROYED back to
NOT_LOADED at once (the ixcdiag run read NOT_LOADED, then STARTED, all
through the hang), and the context is released only after the
caller's own stop. Logged at ERROR ("invoke(): callee destroyed during
remote execution, call abandoned") so a gate can count it.

---
--- a/src/libbluray/bdj/java/org/videolan/BDJAppProxy.java
+++ b/src/libbluray/bdj/java/org/videolan/BDJAppProxy.java
@@ -306,6 +306,7 @@
         if ((state != NOT_LOADED) && (state != LOADED)) {
             try {
                 xlet.destroyXlet(force);
+                context.noteXletEnded();
 
                 context.closeSockets();
                 context.getThreadGroup().waitForShutdown(1000, 1 + context.numEventQueueThreads());
@@ -444,6 +445,7 @@
                 ret = doResume();
                 break;
             case AppCommand.CMD_NOTIFY_DESTROYED:
+                context.noteXletEnded();
                 toState = DESTROYED;
                 state = DESTROYED;
                 ret = true;
--- a/src/libbluray/bdj/java/org/videolan/BDJXletContext.java
+++ b/src/libbluray/bdj/java/org/videolan/BDJXletContext.java
@@ -196,6 +196,20 @@
         return AppsDatabase.getAppsDatabase().getAppProxy(appid);
     }
 
+    /* An xlet of this context ended (its destroyXlet returned, it notified its own destruction, or
+     * the context exited): bumped at each, so an Ixc call into the xlet instance it was made to
+     * can tell that instance is gone even when the proxy is reused (DESTROYED becomes NOT_LOADED
+     * at once) and the context is not released yet. */
+    protected void noteXletEnded() {
+        xletEnds++;
+    }
+
+    protected int getXletEnds() {
+        return xletEnds;
+    }
+
+    private volatile int xletEnds = 0;
+
     public boolean isReleased() {
         return released;
     }
@@ -488,6 +502,8 @@
         // Called from AppProxy when destroyXlet() has been called.
         // Release as much resources as possible while running in Xlet context.
 
+        noteXletEnded();
+
         org.dvb.io.ixc.IxcRegistry.unbindAll(this);
 
         closeSockets();
--- a/src/libbluray/bdj/java/org/videolan/IxcRegistryImpl.java
+++ b/src/libbluray/bdj/java/org/videolan/IxcRegistryImpl.java
@@ -236,8 +236,26 @@
 
             remoteMethod.callerContext.addIxcThread(remoteThread);
             remoteThread.setDaemon(false);
+            /* The callee xlet instance this call goes into: if it ends during the call (or its
+             * context is released) the call never answers. Fail then, as a call into a destroyed
+             * callee does above, instead of holding the caller (and its xlet's stop) until the
+             * caller is killed. */
+            final BDJXletContext callee = remoteObj.context;
+            final int calleeEnds = callee.getXletEnds();
             remoteThread.start();
-            remoteThread.join();
+            while (true) {
+                remoteThread.join(100);
+                if (!remoteThread.isAlive()) {
+                    break;
+                }
+                if (callee.isReleased() || callee.getXletEnds() != calleeEnds) {
+                    logger.error("invoke(): callee destroyed during remote execution, call abandoned: " +
+                                 method + " (callee " + callee + ")");
+                    remoteThread.interrupt();
+                    remoteMethod.callerContext.removeIxcThread(remoteThread);
+                    throw new RemoteException("callee destroyed during remote execution");
+                }
+            }
             remoteMethod.callerContext.removeIxcThread(remoteThread);
 
             if (!remoteMethod.finished) {
